@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .

#
# prima-materia — the excuse that stops at the officiant
#
# One generalization, formed from three attested claims of one transmission.
# It is filed on its own and not with the displaced penalties, which it
# resembles and is not: there the cost of a fault is moved away from the
# person who committed it, here the relief is granted to one party and
# withheld from another, and the question is who may plead that he did not
# know.
#
# The counter-search against the holding was carried before the node was
# written, as SPEC §16 requires, and it is set out in the notes. It did not
# refute the statement and it did fix its wording twice.
#

pmc:TheExcuseThatStopsAtTheOfficiant a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "The excuse of not knowing stops at the officiant"@en, "Die Entschuldigung des Nichtwissens endet beim Vollzieher"@de ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Where a transmission attaches a penalty to a defilement that arose during its own procedure through the body or the act of a participant who is not the officiant, it may relieve that participant on the ground that he did not know or did not will what he did, and it charges the officiant the stated penalty in every case, admitting no corresponding ground on his side even where the fact that triggered the penalty was one he could not have established and the event one he could not have prevented."@en ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:MandaeanInfansSineScientia, pmc:MandaeanUxorisTamia, pmc:MandaeanPaisaq ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    skos:note "The three cases, which are three different participants and one rule. An infant soils itself during its baptism: the transmission states in so many words that its baptism is not invalidated, because it sinned unwittingly, and the priest must nevertheless go through a complete baptism at the hands of a ganzibra and a priest. A candidate's wife menstruates, miscarries or gives birth during his consecration: the pollution invalidates the consecration and everyone concerned in it, beginning with three hundred and sixty-six baptisms for the officiating ganzibra, and nothing is said of her. Matrons examine a bride and are mistaken about her virginity, which the record says happens: the priest who then married her is debarred until he has received fifty full baptisms and the ganzibra three hundred and sixty, and the matrons are not mentioned again."@en ;
    skos:note "What makes this a statement rather than a description of three accidents is the argument the transmission itself supplies in the first case and does not extend. It knows the distinction between a defilement willed and one not willed, it names it, and it applies it to the one participant who by no construction could be held to a standard — an infant — while the officiant, who was equally unable to prevent the event, is charged. The relief is available in principle and is not on offer to him."@en ;
    skos:note "What would refute it, and it is a cheap thing to look for in any manual of ritual faults: a passage relieving an officiant of a prescribed penalty on the ground that he did not know of the defilement or could not have prevented it. One such passage settles it, because the statement is about what a transmission provides and not about what officiants did. The nearest place to look first is the part of the Alf Trisar Šuialia titled blow and healing, which is the Mandaean book of faults and their remedies and could not be obtained in the run that wrote this; see pmp:MandaeanManualisAccessusSearch."@en ;
    skos:note "The counter-search over the compiled holding was carried in two directions. Looking for transmissions that excuse an officiant on the ground of ignorance: nothing. Looking for transmissions that handle unwilled defilement at all, three things came back and none of them falls inside the statement. The Hawaiian hula company restores a member defiled by contact with a corpse through a cleansing with sea water and turmeric, and a wilful transgression is not covered by it and requires penitence and confession: the transmission grades by will, exactly as the statement says such transmissions may, but it says nothing about the teacher who performs the cleansing, so it carries the antecedent and is silent on the consequent. The Fon material runs the other way and shows that the relief is not automatic anywhere: a man who kills a python of the sacred kind, even by accident, is by custom liable to be burned alive. And the Atharvavedic hymns carry the petition form — if knowing, if unknowing, we have committed sins, free us from them — which is a prayer about the state of souls and not a rule about the state of a rite. The Mandaean prayerbook carries the same petition in the same words and likewise states no rule; see pmp:MandaeanQolastaIrritumSearch."@en ;
    skos:note "The counter-search changed the wording twice and the changes are worth keeping. The Fon case removed any claim that the unwilled defilement is generally excused: the statement now says only that the transmission may relieve that participant, because plainly some do not. The Hawaiian case removed the word always from the second clause and put in its place the scope condition that the transmission be one which charges the officiant at all, since a transmission silent about the officiant neither confirms nor refutes."@en ;
    skos:note "The limit of the derivation, stated because it is the weakness a later reader would otherwise have to find. All three witnesses come from one transmission, and all three reach the graph through one recorder, who reports two of them as rules she was told and the third as a rule with two named cases attached. What the three establish is therefore a property of Mandaean priestly practice as it was recorded in Iraq in the nineteen-thirties. Widening it does not mean restating it more boldly: it means opening another manual of ritual faults — the Zoroastrian Nērangestān, the expiation books of the Śrautasūtras, the Chedasūtras of the Jain penance order — and asking the one question above of each. All three are named in the holding and none of them is opened."@en ;
    skos:note "Its relation to pmc:ThePenaltyLaidOnAnotherParty is neighbourhood and not containment, and the two are easy to confuse. That statement is about where a cost lands: away from the performer, on a family, a lineage, the dead, a class of beings. This one is about who may plead. They can hold at once and in the Mandaean material they do: the same corpus that refuses the officiant the plea of ignorance also, at Ginzā 15,1, calls a father to account for a daughter who went to the soothsayers. A transmission can move the cost and withhold the excuse, and the two devices leave the same person paying for something he did not do."@en .
